1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
Agents can be autonomous to different degrees.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:09,860
A few years ago, I noticed within the AI community that there was a growing controversial debate about what is an agent,

3
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:13,620
and some people are writing a paper saying I've built an agent, and others will say,

4
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:17,960
no, that's not really a true agent. And I felt this debate was unnecessary,

5
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:24,100
which is why I started using the term agentic, because I thought if we use it as an adjective

6
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:28,540
rather than a binary, it's either an agent or not, then we're going to have just acknowledged

7
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:34,520
that systems can be agentic to different degrees. And let's just call out agentic and move on with the

8
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:39,200
real work of building these systems rather than debating, you know, is this sufficiently autonomous

9
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:45,760
to be an agent or not? I remember when I prepared a talk on agentic reasoning, one of my team members

10
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:49,900
actually came to me and said, hey, Andrew, we don't need yet another word. You know, we have agent,

11
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:55,120
why are you making up another word agentic? But I decided to use it anyway. And then later on,

12
00:00:55,120 --> 00:01:01,020
wrote an article in a givenized newsletter at the back, and then also posted on social media,

13
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:06,200
saying that instead of arguing over which work to include or exclude as being a true agent,

14
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:11,680
let's acknowledge that different degrees to which systems can be agentic. And I think this helped

15
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:18,580
move past the debate on what is a true agent and let us just focus on actually building them. Some

16
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:24,920
agents can be less autonomous. So take the example of writing an essay about black holes,

17
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:31,660
you can have a relatively simple agents to come up with a few web search terms or web search queries,

18
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:38,200
then you can hard code in that you call a web search engine, fetch some web pages, and then use that

19
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:43,500
to write an essay. This will be an example of a less autonomous agent with a fully deterministic

20
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:49,540
sequence of steps, and this will work okay. In terms of notational convention, throughout this course,

21
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:55,820
I'll use the red color, as you see here on the left, to denote the user input, such as a user query in this

22
00:01:55,820 --> 00:02:03,140
case, or in later examples, maybe the input document into an agentic workflow. The gray boxes denote calls to an

23
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:10,360
LLM, and the green boxes, like the web search and the web fetch boxes that you see here, indicate steps

24
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:16,440
where other software is being used to carry out an action, such as a web search API call or executing code

25
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:22,940
to fetch the contents of a website. Then an agent can be more autonomous, where given a request to write

26
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:28,240
an essay about black holes, perhaps you let the LLM decide does it want to do a web search, or does it

27
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:33,840
want to search recent news sources, or does it want to search for recent research papers on the website

28
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:39,880
archive. Based on that, maybe in this example, the LLM, not the human engineer, but the LLM chooses in

29
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:45,720
this case to call a web search engine, and then after that you may let the LLM decide how many web pages

30
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:52,000
does it want to fetch, or if it fetch the PDF, does it need to call a function or also called a tool

31
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,420
to convert the PDF to text. And in this case, maybe it fetches its top few web pages, then it can

32
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:03,180
write an essay, decide what are to reflect and improve, and maybe even go back to fetch more

33
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:09,520
web pages, and then to finally produce an output. And so even for this example of a research agent,

34
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:16,820
we can see that some agents can be less autonomous with a linear sequence of steps to be executed,

35
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:21,840
determined by a programmer, and some can be more autonomous where you trust the LLM to make more

36
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:26,880
decisions, and the exact sequence of steps that happens may be even determined by the LLM rather than

37
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:33,100
in advance by the programmer. So for less autonomous systems, you will usually have all the steps

38
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:38,620
predetermined in advance, and any functions it calls like web search, and we'll call that tool use as

39
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:43,540
you learn in the third module in this course, might be hard-coded by the human engineer, by you or me,

40
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:49,020
and most of the autonomy is in what text the LLM generates. At the end of the spectrum would be

41
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:54,160
highly autonomous agents, where the agent makes many decisions autonomously, including, for example,

42
00:03:54,320 --> 00:04:00,060
deciding what is the sequence of steps it will carry out in order to write the essay. And there's some

43
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:05,040
highly autonomous agents that can even write new functions, or we sometimes say create new tools

44
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:10,900
that it can then execute. And somewhere in between are semi-autonomous agents, where can make some

45
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:15,900
decisions, choose tools, but the tools are usually more predefined. As you look at different examples

46
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:21,100
in this course, you learn how to build applications anywhere on this spectrum of less to more highly

47
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:25,360
autonomous, and you find that there are tons of applications in the less autonomous end of the

48
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:29,960
spectrum. They're very valuable being built for tons of businesses today, and at the same

49
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:34,720
time, there are also applications being worked on at the more highly autonomous end of the spectrum,

50
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:41,680
but those are usually less easily controllable, a little bit more unpredictable, and also a lot of

51
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:46,120
active research as well to figure out how to build these more highly autonomous agents.

52
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:53,920
And with that, let's go on to the next video to dive deeper into this and to hear about some of the

53
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:58,880
benefits of using agents and why they allow us to do things that just were not possible with

54
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:01,520
of early generations of own base applications.

